3. Implicit Intent: Mapping Goals to Modalities

Without format words, Super Agent infers suitable outputs through LLM, following relatively stable mappings:
Intent pattern  Modality  Reasoning"Compare / contrast / list N"  Inline conversational table  Multiple objects/dimensions suit tables
"Explain to boss / present to client / pitch / train"  AI Slides (PPT)  Presenting/explaining implies paged visuals
"Count / calculate / sum / trends / organize data"  AI Sheets  Calculations imply spreadsheet cells
"Write in detail / draft / review / author"  AI Docs (long document)  Writing/drafting implies long form
"What does X look like / generate a scene"  AI Image  Seeing/scenes imply visuals
"Film an introduction / make promotion"  AI Video  Filming/promotion implies motion
"Build an X tool / implement X"  AI Developer  Technical building/implementation implies code
"Investigate / survey / understand deeply"  Deep Research → Sparkpage  Research needs long content + citations + structure
"Contact / communicate / follow up with X"  Call For Me / email  Contact implies external action
"Explain / what is / why"  Plain conversation (structured text)  Explanations need no document artifact

4. Checkpoint Triggers: Clarification for Vague Intent

With completely vague input (action without format or goal), Super Agent proactively asks. This key design lets users choose instead of risking the wrong modality.

Typical Clarification Scenarios
Vague input  Super Agent choices"Organize the competitor analysis"  [📊 PPT for presentation] [📑 Word report for archiving] [📈 Comparison table for inspection] [💬 List it here]
"Make something for the client"  [📊 Presentation PPT] [📑 Proposal document] [🎨 Poster] [📹 Introduction video]
"Help with tomorrow's meeting"  [📑 Agenda document] [📊 Opening PPT] [💬 Key points] [📞 Call to confirm]
Three Clarification Design Rules

- Usually 3-5 options, no more than 5, avoiding choice overload
- Emoji + short label + one-line purpose (presentation/archiving) makes differences immediately clear
- Keep Tell me more or List it here fallbacks so users are not forced to choose

5. Explicit Selection: Routing-Bypass Shortcuts

Users can bypass Super Agent and choose a modality directly, suitable when they know what they want.

Two Explicit Selection Methods

- Sidebar: Dashboard lists all Agents (AI Slides / AI Sheets / AI Docs / AI Developer / AI Designer, etc.); clicking enters the dedicated modality UI
- Direct URL: each Agent has its own address (e.g. `genspark.ai/agents?type=slides_agent`) for direct navigation

Smart routing serves novices unsure what to use; explicit selection serves experienced users with clear goals. Both cover different stages.

6. Multiple Modalities in One Sentence (Multiple Tags)

One advanced Super Agent capability triggers multiple modalities in a pipeline from one input, fundamentally distinguishing it from single-Agent tools.

Typical Multi-Tag Examples
Input  Modality sequence"Research SaaS and make a PPT for my boss"  `[RESEARCH]` → `[SLIDES]`
"Compare 3 hotels, then call the best to confirm family rooms"  `[RESEARCH]` → `[SHEETS]` (comparison) → `[CALL]`
"Find 20 YouTube AI videos, make a table, then write analysis"  `[RESEARCH]` → `[SHEETS]` → `[DOCS]`
"Make a product introduction video: script, cover, then edit video"  `[DOCS]` (script) → `[IMAGE]` (cover) → `[VIDEO]`
During Plan, Super Agent presents the full pipeline and modality per step so users see the complete path before execution.

7. Key Observations and Design Implications

Keywords are the fast path and intent is the smart path: precise format words use direct matching without LLM semantics; goal-only requests use inference. Together they provide speed and intelligence.
Tags decouple decisions from generation: the LLM recognizes a PPT request without knowing how to draw slides. Adding modalities needs only a tag + endpoint, without changing the LLM.
Clarification respects users more than guessing: 3-5 buttons with one-line explanations make choice quick, avoiding wrong AI decisions and credit waste from repeated format changes.
Explicit shortcuts serve experts: routing serves novices; experts reach AI Slides faster from the sidebar. Both cover the maturity spectrum.
One sentence chains multiple modalities: "research then make a PPT for my boss" triggers `[RESEARCH]` → `[SLIDES]` without intermediate user intervention.
Trigger keywords cover Chinese/English, synonyms, industry terms, and colloquial expressions, enabling correct routing regardless of habitual wording.
